speechtext:
I resent the innuendo of the hon. member for Renfrew North (Mr. Warren) in connection with the Cultus Lake camp. May I explain that I was asked to go to this camp in order to make what contribution I could toward ironing out the difficulties which had arisen. It was thought that perhaps these men would listen to me. These men came from Ontario, Quebec and the maritime provinces. They had left the eastern part of the country in a search for work and had been caught in Vancouver because of their enterprise in searching for work. The majority of them did not want to go to the camp; they did not want relief. They said that all they wanted was some kind of work at decent wages. That was all they were looking for. But no such work was available in British Columbia at that 71492-183}
time. It certainly could not be found in the logging camps or on the farms of British Columbia, especially at that season of the year. There was no direction in which we could turn to find work for these men. On several occasions I queried the officials of the employment service as to whether there was work anywhere in British Columbia, as indicated by their reports from all points in the province, but they told me they could not do anything about it; that there was no work.
What were these men to do? Starve? The local welfare agencies did what they could. In recognition of the fact that there was no work, the federal and provincial governments reluctantly opened up the forestry camps. The majority of these men did splendid work in the camps. I daresay there were some misfits, but having regard to the conditions that have existed during the last five or six years, and the way these men have been kicked about, you could not help but have some misfits. Some of these men have lost all hope; they feel they would rather go to gaol than endure what they have had to endure. Look what happened here the other night. These men contend that nothing could be worse than the conditions which they have had to endure. May I say that I have been in touch with committees of business men who have made a most earnest endeavour to get work for these men. They were making endeavours at that particular time, but they could not secure any work. In every instance that I know of where work was made available, the men grasped the opportunity and most of them made good.
